    // Climate 1, the northern and eastern plains and central India: the
    // demand peak comes before the monsoon, in May, with an air-conditioned
    // afternoon and a second peak late in the hot night; the humid monsoon
    // keeps the night load high; winter has a morning peak and a lighting
    // evening peak over low nights. Climate 2, the west coast, Gujarat and
    // the peninsula: milder seasons, an April and May high, and an evening
    // peak all year. Climate 3, the Himalaya (Srinagar, Kathmandu and
    // Thimphu): a winter heating evening peak. The hourly shapes, the
    // month-to-shape assignment and the monthly factors are authoring
    // judgments on each city's local clock (no sourced hourly load profile
    // was found). The May peak follows India's record demand of 250 GW met on
    // 30 May 2024, with a non-solar-hours record of 234.3 GW on 29 May 2024
    // that marks the evening ramp as solar falls (Ministry of Power statement,
    // reprinted by pv magazine India, "India's Power Sector Achieves Record
    // 250 GW Demand met on 30th May", read 2026-10-25).
